> > > I can tell you what the difference is just to clarify it, but it should work either way and indeed it does in many instances. rmr2 saves two environments: the local environment inside the mapreduce call, which contains things like the map and reduce functions, and the global environment, which is not saved by the first call even if it is an enclosing environment for some of the objects therein. So when the map argument contains a global object, it needs to access the global env, which is saved separately. This is the difference between the copy and paste run and your preferred version (which I agree with you is the only one acceptable from a sw engineering point of view). Now to the difference between mapreduce and equijoin. Equijoin calls mapreduce with a map function that calls map.left and map.right. So there is one additional level of indirection. It seems that when this additional level of indirection is present, global environment objects become unavailable, as your second example confirms.
